```

**Logic**

Each term describes the previous one by reading off runs: "1211" is read as one 1, one 2, two 1s, giving "111221". So generating term n means applying a run-length encoding n-1 times starting from "1".

The encoding itself is a single pass: count consecutive equal characters, then append the count followed by the character.

The terms grow roughly geometrically, by a factor of about 1.3 each time, so this is only practical for small n. That growth rate is a known constant, and it is a good thing to mention.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text;

public class Solution
{
    public static string CountAndSay(int n)
    {
        if (n <= 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(n));

        string current = "1";
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i++) current = Encode(current);
        return current;
    }

    // Run-length encode: count then character, for each run.
    private static string Encode(string s)
    {
        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        int i = 0;

        while (i < s.Length)
        {
            char c = s[i];
            int count = 0;

            while (i < s.Length && s[i] == c) { i++; count++; }

            sb.Append(count).Append(c);
        }

        return sb.ToString();
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        for (int n = 1; n <= 6; n++) Console.WriteLine(CountAndSay(n));

        Console.WriteLine(Encode("aaabbc"));
        Console.WriteLine(CountAndSay(10).Length);   // growth is geometric
    }
}
```

