```

**Approach**

The `*` is what makes this hard: it binds to the character before it and can match zero or more occurrences, so a greedy scan cannot work. Dynamic programming over both strings handles it cleanly.

**Logic**

`match[i][j]` means the first i characters of the input match the first j of the pattern.

For a plain character or `.`, the answer is the diagonal, provided the current characters agree. For `*`, there are two cases: use it zero times, which skips the pair and looks two columns back; or use it once more, which requires the preceding pattern character to match the current input character and then looks one row up.

The base row needs care: an empty input matches a pattern like `a*b*` because every starred pair can contribute nothing.

O(n·m) time and space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool IsMatch(string s, string p)
    {
        int n = s.Length, m = p.Length;
        var match = new bool[n + 1, m + 1];
        match[0, 0] = true;

        // Empty input can still match a pattern made of x* pairs.
        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++)
            if (p[j - 1] == '*') match[0, j] = match[0, j - 2];

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++)
            {
                if (p[j - 1] == '*')
                {
                    // zero occurrences: skip the pair entirely
                    match[i, j] = match[i, j - 2];

                    // one more occurrence: the char before * must match s[i-1]
                    if (p[j - 2] == '.' || p[j - 2] == s[i - 1])
                        match[i, j] = match[i, j] || match[i - 1, j];
                }
                else if (p[j - 1] == '.' || p[j - 1] == s[i - 1])
                {
                    match[i, j] = match[i - 1, j - 1];
                }
            }

        return match[n, m];
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("aa", "a"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("aa", "a*"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("ab", ".*"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("mississippi", "mis*is*p*."));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("", "a*b*"));      // empty matches optional pairs
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("aab", "c*a*b"));  // c* contributes nothing
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
False
True
True
False
True
True
```

**Logic**

Prefix sums with a hash map. If the running sum up to index j is `S`, then a subarray ending at j sums to k exactly when some earlier prefix equalled `S - k`. So keep a map from prefix sum to how many times it has occurred, and at each step add the count of `S - k`.

Seed the map with `{0: 1}` to represent the empty prefix, which is what lets a subarray starting at index 0 be counted. O(n) time, O(n) space.

The sliding window does not work here, because negative numbers mean the running sum is not monotonic and shrinking the window can increase the sum.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int SubarraySum(int[] nums, int k)
    {
        // {0:1} is the empty prefix: it makes a subarray starting at index 0 countable.
        var seen = new Dictionary<long, int> { [0] = 1 };
        long sum = 0;
        int count = 0;

        foreach (int n in nums)
        {
            sum += n;

            // Any earlier prefix equal to sum-k closes a subarray summing to k here.
            if (seen.TryGetValue(sum - k, out int times)) count += times;

            seen[sum] = seen.TryGetValue(sum, out int c) ? c + 1 : 1;
        }

        return count;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { 1, 1, 1 }, 2));
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { 1, 2, 3 }, 3));         // [1,2] and [3]
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { 1 }, 0));
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { -1, -1, 1 }, 0));       // negatives
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { 3, 4, 7, 2, -3, 1, 4, 2 }, 7));
        Console.WriteLine(SubarraySum(new[] { 0, 0, 0 }, 0));         // every subarray counts
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
2
2
0
1
4
6
```

**Logic**

Put everything in a hash set, then for each value check whether it is the *start* of a run by asking if `value - 1` is absent. Only from a genuine start do you walk upward counting. That test is what keeps it linear: without it, a long run would be walked from every one of its members, making it quadratic.

Each value is visited at most twice overall, once in the outer loop and once during the single walk of its run, so O(n) time and O(n) space. Sorting would be O(n log n), which the problem is asking you to beat.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int LongestConsecutive(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length == 0) return 0;

        var set = new HashSet<int>(nums);   // also removes duplicates
        int best = 0;

        foreach (int n in set)
        {
            // Only start counting from the beginning of a run.
            if (set.Contains(n - 1)) continue;

            int current = n, length = 1;
            while (set.Contains(current + 1)) { current++; length++; }

            if (length > best) best = length;
        }

        return best;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new[] { 100, 4, 200, 1, 3, 2 }));   // 1,2,3,4
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new[] { 0, 3, 7, 2, 5, 8, 4, 6, 0, 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new int[0]));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new[] { 5 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new[] { 1, 1, 1 }));      // duplicates collapse
        Console.WriteLine(LongestConsecutive(new[] { -3, -2, -1, 0 })); // negatives
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
