**Logic**

With a bounded alphabet, a bitmask replaces the hash set: one bit per character, tested and set as you scan. A repeat is detected when its bit is already set. That is O(n) time and a single integer of space.

For 26 lowercase letters an `int` suffices; for extended ASCII you would need a `long` pair or four. If the string is longer than the alphabet, the pigeonhole principle guarantees a duplicate, so it can be rejected without looking at the characters at all.

The truly no-extra-space option is sorting and comparing neighbours, at O(n log n), which mutates the input.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    // Assumes lowercase a-z. One bit per letter.
    public static bool AllUnique(string s)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return true;

        // More characters than the alphabet means a repeat must exist.
        if (s.Length > 26) return false;

        int seen = 0;

        foreach (char c in s)
        {
            int bit = 1 << (c - 'a');

            if ((seen & bit) != 0) return false;   // already set: duplicate
            seen |= bit;
        }

        return true;
    }

    // No assumption about the alphabet, but it reorders the caller's data.
    public static bool AllUniqueBySorting(char[] chars)
    {
        Array.Sort(chars);
        for (int i = 1; i < chars.Length; i++)
            if (chars[i] == chars[i - 1]) return false;
        return true;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(AllUnique("abcdef"));
        Console.WriteLine(AllUnique("hello"));
        Console.WriteLine(AllUnique(""));
        Console.WriteLine(AllUnique("a"));
        Console.WriteLine(AllUnique(new string('a', 27)));    // rejected on length alone
        Console.WriteLine(AllUniqueBySorting("Hello World".ToCharArray()));
    }
}
```

