2
4
2
```

**Approach**

Wildcard matching, where `?` matches exactly one character and `*` matches any sequence including empty. Unlike regex `*`, this star is standalone rather than bound to a preceding character, which makes the recurrence simpler.

**Logic**

`match[i][j]` means the first i characters of the input match the first j of the pattern.

For `?` or an equal character, carry the diagonal. For `*`, two options: it matches nothing, which looks one column back, or it consumes one more input character, which looks one row up. Either suffices, so they are combined with an OR.

The base row matters: an empty input matches a pattern of stars only.

O(n·m) time and space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool IsMatch(string s, string p)
    {
        int n = s.Length, m = p.Length;
        var match = new bool[n + 1, m + 1];
        match[0, 0] = true;

        // An empty input matches only a run of stars.
        for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++)
            if (p[j - 1] == '*') match[0, j] = match[0, j - 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
            for (int j = 1; j <= m; j++)
            {
                if (p[j - 1] == '*')
                {
                    // star matches nothing, or consumes one more character
                    match[i, j] = match[i, j - 1] || match[i - 1, j];
                }
                else if (p[j - 1] == '?' || p[j - 1] == s[i - 1])
                {
                    match[i, j] = match[i - 1, j - 1];
                }
            }

        return match[n, m];
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("aa", "a"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("aa", "*"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("cb", "?a"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("adceb", "*a*b"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("acdcb", "a*c?b"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("", "***"));       // stars can match nothing
    }
}
```

