**Approach**

Backtracking over an increasing sequence, so each combination is generated exactly once rather than in every permutation.

**Logic**

At each level choose the next number strictly greater than the previous one. That ordering is what distinguishes a combination from a permutation: `[1,2]` is produced but `[2,1]` never is.

The useful prune is to stop the loop early when not enough numbers remain to complete the combination. If k-c more are needed and only n-i+1 remain, the branch cannot succeed and exploring it is wasted. Without that prune the search explores a large number of dead ends for k close to n.

Output size is n choose k, which is optimal.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution
{
    public static IList<IList<int>> Combine(int n, int k)
    {
        var result = new List<IList<int>>();
        if (k <= 0 || k > n) return result;

        Backtrack(1, n, k, new List<int>(), result);
        return result;
    }

    private static void Backtrack(int start, int n, int k,
                                  List<int> current, IList<IList<int>> result)
    {
        if (current.Count == k) { result.Add(new List<int>(current)); return; }

        int needed = k - current.Count;

        // Stop when too few numbers remain to finish: n - i + 1 < needed.
        for (int i = start; i <= n - needed + 1; i++)
        {
            current.Add(i);
            Backtrack(i + 1, n, k, current, result);   // i + 1 keeps it increasing
            current.RemoveAt(current.Count - 1);
        }
    }

    private static string Render(IList<IList<int>> r) =>
        r.Count == 0 ? "(none)" : string.Join(" ", r.Select(c => "[" + string.Join(",", c) + "]"));

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Render(Combine(4, 2)));
        Console.WriteLine(Render(Combine(3, 3)));
        Console.WriteLine(Render(Combine(3, 1)));
        Console.WriteLine(Render(Combine(3, 0)));      // k of zero
        Console.WriteLine(Render(Combine(2, 5)));      // k larger than n
        Console.WriteLine(Combine(20, 10).Count);      // 20 choose 10
    }
}
```

