**Approach**

An unbounded coin-change DP where the coin denominations are the perfect squares up to n.

**Logic**

`best[i]` is the fewest squares summing to i. For each value, try every square that fits and take one more than the best for the remainder.

The inner loop runs while `square * square <= i`, so the total work is the sum of square roots, giving O(n·√n). That is efficient enough for large n and is the expected answer.

Initialising to `i` rather than to infinity is a small convenience: any number can always be made from that many ones, so it is a valid upper bound that also avoids overflow when adding one.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int NumSquares(int n)
    {
        if (n <= 0) return 0;

        var best = new int[n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
        {
            best[i] = i;   // upper bound: i ones. Also avoids an infinity sentinel.

            for (int root = 1; root * root <= i; root++)
                best[i] = Math.Min(best[i], best[i - root * root] + 1);
        }

        return best[n];
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(NumSquares(12));    // 4 + 4 + 4
        Console.WriteLine(NumSquares(13));    // 4 + 9
        Console.WriteLine(NumSquares(1));
        Console.WriteLine(NumSquares(0));
        Console.WriteLine(NumSquares(16));    // itself a square
        Console.WriteLine(NumSquares(7));     // 4 + 1 + 1 + 1
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
3
2
1
0
1
4
```

**Approach**

A DP keyed on the pair (index, common difference). For each pair of indices, the difference between them extends whatever run already ended at the earlier index with that same difference.

**Logic**

`best[i][d]` is the length of the longest arithmetic subsequence ending at index i with common difference d. Since d can be any integer, including negative, a dictionary per index is used rather than an array.

For each j before i, the difference is `nums[i] - nums[j]`. If a run with that difference already ends at j, extend it; otherwise start a new run of length 2, since two elements always form an arithmetic sequence.

O(n²) time and space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int LongestArithSeqLength(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length == 0) return 0;
        if (nums.Length <= 2) return nums.Length;

        // One dictionary per index: the difference can be any integer.
        var best = new Dictionary<int, int>[nums.Length];
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++) best[i] = new Dictionary<int, int>();

        int longest = 2;

        for (int i = 1; i < nums.Length; i++)
            for (int j = 0; j < i; j++)
            {
                int difference = nums[i] - nums[j];

                // Extend a run ending at j, or start a new pair.
                int length = best[j].TryGetValue(difference, out int previous) ? previous + 1 : 2;

                best[i][difference] = Math.Max(best[i].GetValueOrDefault(difference), length);
                longest = Math.Max(longest, best[i][difference]);
            }

        return longest;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(LongestArithSeqLength(new[] { 3, 6, 9, 12 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestArithSeqLength(new[] { 9, 4, 7, 2, 10 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestArithSeqLength(new[] { 20, 1, 15, 3, 10, 5, 8 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestArithSeqLength(new[] { 5 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestArithSeqLength(new[] { 7, 7, 7, 7 }));   // difference of 0
        Console.WriteLine(LongestArithSeqLength(new[] { 1, 2 }));
    }
}
