**Approach**

A two-dimensional DP counting, for each prefix pair, how many ways the prefix of t can be formed as a subsequence of the prefix of s.

**Logic**

`ways[i][j]` is the number of ways to form the first j characters of t from the first i of s.

If the current characters differ, the character of s cannot be used, so the count carries over from `ways[i-1][j]`. If they match, you may either use it, adding `ways[i-1][j-1]`, or skip it, adding `ways[i-1][j]`. Both options are counted because they produce distinct subsequences.

The base case is that an empty t can be formed exactly one way from any prefix of s: take nothing.

O(n·m) time, reducible to O(m) space with a single row iterated backwards.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static long NumDistinct(string s, string t)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(t)) return 1;              // one way: take nothing
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s) || s.Length < t.Length) return 0;

        // One row over t, iterated BACKWARDS so ways[j-1] is still the previous row.
        var ways = new long[t.Length + 1];
        ways[0] = 1;

        foreach (char c in s)
            for (int j = t.Length; j >= 1; j--)
                if (t[j - 1] == c) ways[j] += ways[j - 1];

        return ways[t.Length];
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(NumDistinct("rabbbit", "rabbit"));
        Console.WriteLine(NumDistinct("babgbag", "bag"));
        Console.WriteLine(NumDistinct("abc", ""));          // empty target
        Console.WriteLine(NumDistinct("", "a"));
        Console.WriteLine(NumDistinct("aaa", "a"));         // three positions
        Console.WriteLine(NumDistinct("aaaa", "aa"));       // choose 2 of 4
    }
}
```

