**Logic**

Three approaches worth knowing. The naive one shifts and tests each of the 32 bits. Brian Kernighan's trick, `n &= n - 1`, clears the lowest set bit each iteration, so it loops only as many times as there are set bits. And in .NET 6 or later, `BitOperations.PopCount` compiles to a single CPU instruction where available.

Subtracting one flips the lowest set bit to zero and sets everything below it, so ANDing with the original clears exactly that bit. That identity underpins several bit tricks.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Numerics;

public class Solution
{
    // Kernighan: loops once per SET bit, not once per bit.
    public static int CountBits(uint n)
    {
        int count = 0;

        while (n != 0)
        {
            n &= n - 1;    // clears the lowest set bit
            count++;
        }

        return count;
    }

    // Naive: always 32 iterations.
    public static int CountBitsNaive(uint n)
    {
        int count = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < 32; i++) if ((n & (1u << i)) != 0) count++;
        return count;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(CountBits(11));            // 1011
        Console.WriteLine(CountBits(128));           // a single bit
        Console.WriteLine(CountBits(0));
        Console.WriteLine(CountBits(uint.MaxValue)); // all 32 set
        Console.WriteLine(CountBitsNaive(11));
        Console.WriteLine(BitOperations.PopCount(11u));   // hardware instruction
    }
}
```

