-2147483648
7
```

**Approach**

XOR everything to get the XOR of the two singles, then use any bit where they differ to split the array into two groups, each containing exactly one of them.

**Logic**

XORing the whole array cancels every paired value and leaves `a ^ b`. That result is non-zero, since a and b differ, so it has at least one set bit — and any such bit is a position where a and b disagree.

Isolate the lowest set bit with `x & -x`. Partition every element by whether it has that bit: a and b land in different groups, and every duplicate pair lands together in one group since both copies share all bits. XOR each group independently and the two answers fall out.

O(n) time, O(1) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int[] SingleNumbers(int[] nums)
    {
        int xorOfBoth = 0;
        foreach (int n in nums) xorOfBoth ^= n;

        // Any set bit here is a position where the two answers differ.
        // x & -x isolates the lowest one.
        int differingBit = xorOfBoth & -xorOfBoth;

        int a = 0, b = 0;

        foreach (int n in nums)
        {
            if ((n & differingBit) != 0) a ^= n;   // duplicates always land together
            else b ^= n;
        }

        return a < b ? new[] { a, b } : new[] { b, a };
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SingleNumbers(new[] { 1, 2, 1, 3, 2, 5 })));
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SingleNumbers(new[] { -1, 0 })));
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SingleNumbers(new[] { 0, 1 })));
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SingleNumbers(new[] { 9, 9, 4, 7 })));
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SingleNumbers(new[] { 2, 2, 100, -100 })));
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", SingleNumbers(new[] { int.MinValue, 5, 5, 3 })));
    }
}
