**Logic**

The greatest common divisor of a and b equals that of b and a modulo b, because any common divisor of a and b also divides their difference and hence the remainder. Repeating until the remainder is zero leaves the answer in the other variable.

It is remarkably fast: the remainder at least halves every two steps, so it is O(log min(a, b)). The worst case is consecutive Fibonacci numbers, which is a pleasing connection.

Least common multiple follows as `a / gcd * b`, dividing first to avoid overflow.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static long Gcd(long a, long b)
    {
        a = Math.Abs(a);
        b = Math.Abs(b);

        while (b != 0)
        {
            (a, b) = (b, a % b);
        }

        return a;   // gcd(0, 0) is 0 by convention
    }

    // Divide before multiplying, or a * b overflows for large inputs.
    public static long Lcm(long a, long b)
    {
        if (a == 0 || b == 0) return 0;
        return Math.Abs(a) / Gcd(a, b) * Math.Abs(b);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Gcd(48, 18));
        Console.WriteLine(Gcd(17, 5));        // coprime
        Console.WriteLine(Gcd(0, 5));
        Console.WriteLine(Gcd(-12, 18));      // sign ignored
        Console.WriteLine(Lcm(4, 6));
        Console.WriteLine(Lcm(1000000000, 999999999));   // would overflow if multiplied first
    }
}
```

