**Logic**

Peel digits off the right with modulo and division, building the reversed value by multiplying by ten and adding. The whole difficulty is overflow: reversing 1534236469 gives a value beyond int range, and the problem requires returning 0 rather than wrapping.

Check *before* multiplying. If the accumulated value already exceeds `int.MaxValue / 10`, the next multiplication overflows. At exactly that boundary, the final digit decides. Checking after the fact is too late, because the overflow has already happened and the value is corrupt.

O(number of digits) time.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int Reverse(int n)
    {
        int result = 0;

        while (n != 0)
        {
            int digit = n % 10;   // C# % keeps the sign, so negatives work unchanged
            n /= 10;

            // Check BEFORE multiplying: afterwards the damage is done.
            if (result > int.MaxValue / 10 ||
                (result == int.MaxValue / 10 && digit > 7)) return 0;

            if (result < int.MinValue / 10 ||
                (result == int.MinValue / 10 && digit < -8)) return 0;

            result = result * 10 + digit;
        }

        return result;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Reverse(123));
        Console.WriteLine(Reverse(-123));         // sign preserved
        Console.WriteLine(Reverse(120));          // trailing zero disappears
        Console.WriteLine(Reverse(0));
        Console.WriteLine(Reverse(1534236469));   // overflows: returns 0
        Console.WriteLine(Reverse(int.MinValue)); // cannot be negated either
    }
}
```

