**Logic**

The process either reaches 1 or falls into a cycle, so detecting repetition is what makes it terminate. A hash set of seen values works, and Floyd's tortoise and hare does the same in O(1) space, since the sequence `x = f(x)` is exactly the shape cycle detection handles.

The reason it always terminates is that the digit-square sum of any number below 1000 is below 250, so the sequence is trapped in a small range and must repeat.

Every unhappy number eventually reaches the same cycle beginning at 4, which gives a third, cheeky solution.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    private static int SquareDigits(int n)
    {
        int sum = 0;
        while (n > 0)
        {
            int digit = n % 10;
            sum += digit * digit;
            n /= 10;
        }
        return sum;
    }

    // Floyd's cycle detection: O(1) space, no set required.
    public static bool IsHappy(int n)
    {
        if (n <= 0) return false;

        int slow = n, fast = n;

        do
        {
            slow = SquareDigits(slow);
            fast = SquareDigits(SquareDigits(fast));
        } while (slow != fast && fast != 1);

        return fast == 1;
    }

    // The obvious version, for comparison.
    public static bool IsHappyWithSet(int n)
    {
        var seen = new HashSet<int>();
        while (n != 1 && seen.Add(n)) n = SquareDigits(n);
        return n == 1;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(IsHappy(19));      // 19 -> 82 -> 68 -> 100 -> 1
        Console.WriteLine(IsHappy(2));       // falls into the 4-cycle
        Console.WriteLine(IsHappy(1));
        Console.WriteLine(IsHappy(7));
        Console.WriteLine(IsHappyWithSet(19));
        Console.WriteLine(IsHappyWithSet(4));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
True
False
True
True
True
False
```

**Logic**

Map each long URL to a short, unique key and store both directions. The key design decision is how the key is generated.

A counter encoded in base 62 gives the shortest possible keys with no collisions ever, since each id is used once. Hashing the URL and truncating is the common alternative but requires collision handling, because truncated hashes will collide.

A second map from URL to key makes the encoder idempotent, so shortening the same URL twice returns the same key rather than leaking a new one each time.

All operations are O(1).

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

public class UrlShortener
{
    private const string Alphabet =
        "0123456789abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ";   // base 62

    private readonly Dictionary<string, string> _keyToUrl = new();
    private readonly Dictionary<string, string> _urlToKey = new();
    private long _nextId = 1;

    public string Encode(string longUrl)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(longUrl)) throw new ArgumentException("url required");

        // Idempotent: the same URL always yields the same key.
        if (_urlToKey.TryGetValue(longUrl, out string existing)) return existing;

        string key = ToBase62(_nextId++);
        _keyToUrl[key] = longUrl;
        _urlToKey[longUrl] = key;

        return key;
    }

    public string Decode(string key) =>
        _keyToUrl.TryGetValue(key, out string url)
            ? url
            : throw new KeyNotFoundException($"unknown key '{key}'");

    private static string ToBase62(long value)
    {
        if (value == 0) return "0";

        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        while (value > 0)
        {
            sb.Insert(0, Alphabet[(int)(value % 62)]);
            value /= 62;
        }
        return sb.ToString();
    }

    public int Count => _keyToUrl.Count;
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var s = new UrlShortener();

        string a = s.Encode("https://example.com/a/very/long/path");
        string b = s.Encode("https://example.com/another");

        Console.WriteLine($"{a} {b}");
        Console.WriteLine(s.Decode(a));
        Console.WriteLine(s.Encode("https://example.com/a/very/long/path") == a);   // idempotent
        Console.WriteLine(s.Count);

        try { s.Decode("nope"); }
        catch (KeyNotFoundException) { Console.WriteLine("unknown key"); }

        // Base 62 stays short as ids grow: 62^6 is over 56 billion.
        var big = new UrlShortener();
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) big.Encode("https://example.com/" + i);
        Console.WriteLine(big.Encode("https://example.com/last").Length);
    }
}
```

