https://example.com/a/very/long/path
True
2
unknown key
2
```

**Logic**

Storing every hit timestamp works but grows without bound under load. Since the window is 300 seconds and the resolution is one second, a fixed circular buffer of 300 slots suffices.

Each slot holds a timestamp and a count. On a hit, if the slot's stored timestamp is stale, it belonged to a previous window, so reset it rather than adding to it. Counting sums only the slots whose timestamps fall inside the window.

O(1) per hit, O(window) per query, and constant memory regardless of traffic.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class HitCounter
{
    private const int WindowSeconds = 300;

    private readonly int[] _timestamps = new int[WindowSeconds];
    private readonly int[] _counts = new int[WindowSeconds];

    public void Hit(int timestamp)
    {
        int slot = timestamp % WindowSeconds;

        if (_timestamps[slot] != timestamp)
        {
            // Stale slot from an earlier window: overwrite rather than accumulate.
            _timestamps[slot] = timestamp;
            _counts[slot] = 1;
        }
        else
        {
            _counts[slot]++;
        }
    }

    public int GetHits(int timestamp)
    {
        int total = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < WindowSeconds; i++)
            if (timestamp - _timestamps[i] < WindowSeconds) total += _counts[i];

        return total;
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var counter = new HitCounter();

        counter.Hit(1);
        counter.Hit(2);
        counter.Hit(3);
        Console.WriteLine(counter.GetHits(4));

        counter.Hit(300);
        Console.WriteLine(counter.GetHits(300));

        // At 301 the hit from second 1 has fallen out of the window.
        Console.WriteLine(counter.GetHits(301));

        // Many hits in the same second collapse into one slot.
        var busy = new HitCounter();
        for (int i = 0; i < 1000; i++) busy.Hit(50);
        Console.WriteLine(busy.GetHits(50));
        Console.WriteLine(busy.GetHits(349));   // still inside the window
        Console.WriteLine(busy.GetHits(350));   // just outside
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
3
4
3
1000
1000
0
```

**Logic**

A window is valid when its length minus the count of its most frequent character is at most k, because everything else must be replaced. Expand the right edge; when the window becomes invalid, move the left edge forward.

The elegant part is that the maximum frequency never needs to be recomputed downwards. Since we only care about the longest window ever seen, letting a stale maximum persist can never produce an answer larger than the true one, so the window simply stops growing until a genuinely better frequency appears. That keeps it O(n) with no inner scan.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int CharacterReplacement(string s, int k)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return 0;

        var counts = new int[26];
        int left = 0, maxCount = 0, best = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < s.Length; right++)
        {
            counts[s[right] - 'A']++;
            maxCount = Math.Max(maxCount, counts[s[right] - 'A']);

            // Everything that is not the dominant character must be replaced.
            while (right - left + 1 - maxCount > k)
            {
                counts[s[left] - 'A']--;
                left++;
                // maxCount is deliberately NOT recomputed: see the follow-up.
            }

            best = Math.Max(best, right - left + 1);
        }

        return best;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(CharacterReplacement("ABAB", 2));
        Console.WriteLine(CharacterReplacement("AABABBA", 1));
        Console.WriteLine(CharacterReplacement("AAAA", 0));     // nothing to replace
        Console.WriteLine(CharacterReplacement("ABCDE", 0));
        Console.WriteLine(CharacterReplacement("A", 5));        // k larger than the string
        Console.WriteLine(CharacterReplacement("ABBB", 2));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
