False
True
False
True
False
```

The last case is the useful trap: every letter of `hello` appears in the text, but never as a contiguous block with the right counts.

**Logic**

Two pointers inward. While the characters match, keep going. On the first mismatch, exactly one deletion is available, and it must remove either the left or the right character, so check whether either resulting substring is a palindrome.

Because only one deletion is allowed, the helper needs no further budget and is a plain palindrome check. That keeps it O(n): one scan to the mismatch, then at most two linear checks.

Trying to be clever and pick a side by lookahead is unsound; both branches must be tested.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool ValidPalindrome(string s)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return true;

        int left = 0, right = s.Length - 1;

        while (left < right)
        {
            if (s[left] != s[right])
            {
                // One deletion left: it removes either side. Both must be tried.
                return IsPalindrome(s, left + 1, right)
                    || IsPalindrome(s, left, right - 1);
            }

            left++;
            right--;
        }

        return true;
    }

    private static bool IsPalindrome(string s, int left, int right)
    {
        while (left < right)
        {
            if (s[left] != s[right]) return false;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
        return true;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(ValidPalindrome("aba"));      // already one
        Console.WriteLine(ValidPalindrome("abca"));     // delete b or c
        Console.WriteLine(ValidPalindrome("abc"));      // needs two deletions
        Console.WriteLine(ValidPalindrome("a"));
        Console.WriteLine(ValidPalindrome("deeee"));    // delete the leading d
        Console.WriteLine(ValidPalindrome("cbbcc"));
    }
}
```

