True
False
```

**Logic**

A bijection between pattern characters and words, so two maps are needed, one in each direction. A single forward map accepts `abba` against `dog dog dog dog`, because every character maps consistently to `dog`; only the reverse map catches that `dog` is already claimed.

Compare the counts before looping: a four-character pattern against three words must be rejected immediately, and doing it first avoids an index error.

O(n) time, O(distinct) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool WordPattern(string pattern, string text)
    {
        if (pattern == null || text == null) return false;

        string[] words = text.Split(' ', StringSplitOptions.RemoveEmptyEntries);
        if (words.Length != pattern.Length) return false;   // check before indexing

        var charToWord = new Dictionary<char, string>();
        var wordToChar = new Dictionary<string, char>();

        for (int i = 0; i < pattern.Length; i++)
        {
            char c = pattern[i];
            string w = words[i];

            bool hasChar = charToWord.TryGetValue(c, out string mappedWord);
            bool hasWord = wordToChar.TryGetValue(w, out char mappedChar);

            if (hasChar != hasWord) return false;            // mapped one way only
            if (hasChar && (mappedWord != w || mappedChar != c)) return false;

            charToWord[c] = w;
            wordToChar[w] = c;
        }

        return true;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(WordPattern("abba", "dog cat cat dog"));
        Console.WriteLine(WordPattern("abba", "dog cat cat fish"));
        Console.WriteLine(WordPattern("aaaa", "dog cat cat dog"));
        Console.WriteLine(WordPattern("abba", "dog dog dog dog"));   // not one-to-one
        Console.WriteLine(WordPattern("ab", "dog cat cat"));         // length mismatch
        Console.WriteLine(WordPattern("", ""));
    }
}
```

