apple
(none)
a
(none)
break
```

**Approach**

One pass tracking the most recent index of each word. Whenever either is seen, the distance to the other's last position is a candidate.

**Logic**

Because only the most recent occurrence of each word can produce the shortest distance to a new occurrence of the other, two integers suffice. There is no need to store all positions.

The same-word variant, where both arguments are identical, needs separate handling: the distance is between consecutive occurrences of that one word, so the previous index is compared against the current one rather than against a different word's index.

O(n) time, O(1) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int ShortestDistance(string[] words, string a, string b)
    {
        if (words == null || words.Length == 0) return -1;

        int lastA = -1, lastB = -1, best = int.MaxValue;
        bool sameWord = a == b;

        for (int i = 0; i < words.Length; i++)
        {
            if (words[i] == a)
            {
                // Same word: the previous occurrence of a IS the other endpoint.
                if (sameWord) { if (lastA >= 0) best = Math.Min(best, i - lastA); lastA = i; continue; }

                lastA = i;
                if (lastB >= 0) best = Math.Min(best, i - lastB);
            }
            else if (words[i] == b)
            {
                lastB = i;
                if (lastA >= 0) best = Math.Min(best, i - lastA);
            }
        }

        return best == int.MaxValue ? -1 : best;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        var words = new[] { "practice", "makes", "perfect", "coding", "makes" };

        Console.WriteLine(ShortestDistance(words, "coding", "practice"));
        Console.WriteLine(ShortestDistance(words, "makes", "coding"));
        Console.WriteLine(ShortestDistance(words, "makes", "makes"));      // same word
        Console.WriteLine(ShortestDistance(words, "missing", "makes"));    // absent
        Console.WriteLine(ShortestDistance(new[] { "a", "b", "a" }, "a", "b"));
        Console.WriteLine(ShortestDistance(Array.Empty<string>(), "a", "b"));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
3
1
3
-1
1
-1
```

**Logic**

The value range matches the index range, so the array can encode its own bookkeeping. For each value v, negate the element at index v-1 as a visited mark. If that element is already negative, v has been seen before, so it is a duplicate.

Use the absolute value when computing the index, since earlier marks may have flipped the sign of the element you are reading. O(n) time, O(1) extra space, and the signs can be restored afterwards if the caller needs the array intact.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static IList<int> FindDuplicates(int[] nums)
    {
        var duplicates = new List<int>();
        if (nums == null) return duplicates;

        foreach (int raw in nums)
        {
            int value = Math.Abs(raw);       // the element may already be negated
            int index = value - 1;

            if (nums[index] < 0) duplicates.Add(value);   // seen before
            else nums[index] = -nums[index];              // mark as seen
        }

        // Restore, so the method does not leave the caller's array negated.
        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++) nums[i] = Math.Abs(nums[i]);

        return duplicates;
    }

    private static string Show(int[] nums)
    {
        var r = FindDuplicates(nums);
        var list = new List<int>(r);
        list.Sort();
        return list.Count == 0 ? "(none)" : string.Join(",", list);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Show(new[] { 4, 3, 2, 7, 8, 2, 3, 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(new[] { 1, 1, 2 }));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(new[] { 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4 }));       // no duplicates

        var original = new[] { 2, 2, 1 };
        FindDuplicates(original);
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", original));       // restored
    }
}
```

