**Approach**

Prefix sums modulo k. Two prefixes with the same remainder bound a subarray whose sum is a multiple of k.

**Logic**

Store the earliest index at which each remainder was seen. When a remainder repeats, the subarray between the two positions sums to a multiple of k. Recording the earliest occurrence maximises the span, which is what allows the length-at-least-two requirement to be met.

Seed the map with remainder 0 at index -1, representing the empty prefix, so a qualifying subarray starting at index 0 is found.

The length check is the trap: the two indices must differ by more than one, or a single element that happens to be a multiple of k would qualify.

O(n) time, O(min(n, k)) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool CheckSubarraySum(int[] nums, int k)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length < 2) return false;

        // remainder -> EARLIEST index. -1 represents the empty prefix.
        var firstSeen = new Dictionary<int, int> { [0] = -1 };
        int running = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            running += nums[i];

            // k of 0 means "sums to zero", so skip the modulo in that case.
            int remainder = k == 0 ? running : ((running % k) + k) % k;

            if (firstSeen.TryGetValue(remainder, out int earlier))
            {
                if (i - earlier > 1) return true;   // length at least two
            }
            else
            {
                firstSeen[remainder] = i;           // record only the first
            }
        }

        return false;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(CheckSubarraySum(new[] { 23, 2, 4, 6, 7 }, 6));     // 2+4
        Console.WriteLine(CheckSubarraySum(new[] { 23, 2, 6, 4, 7 }, 13));    // total is 42, not a multiple
        Console.WriteLine(CheckSubarraySum(new[] { 23, 2, 6, 4, 7 }, 6));
        Console.WriteLine(CheckSubarraySum(new[] { 1, 0 }, 2));               // not a multiple
        Console.WriteLine(CheckSubarraySum(new[] { 0, 0 }, 0));               // k of zero
        Console.WriteLine(CheckSubarraySum(new[] { 6 }, 6));                  // too short
    }
}
