**Approach**

Sort tallest first, breaking ties by the count ascending, then insert each person at the index given by their count.

**Logic**

The insight is that inserting in descending height order makes each insertion safe. When a person is placed, everyone already in the list is at least as tall, so the count of taller people in front is exactly the number of people before them — which is the index. Shorter people inserted later do not affect that count, because they are not taller.

Sorting ties by count ascending matters: among equal heights, the one expecting fewer people in front must be placed first, or the later insertion would displace it.

O(n²) because of the list insertions.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution
{
    // people[i] = [height, countOfTallerOrEqualInFront]
    public static int[][] ReconstructQueue(int[][] people)
    {
        if (people == null || people.Length == 0) return Array.Empty<int[]>();

        // Tallest first; among equals, the smaller count first.
        var sorted = people.OrderByDescending(p => p[0]).ThenBy(p => p[1]).ToArray();

        var queue = new List<int[]>();

        foreach (var person in sorted)
        {
            // Everyone already placed is at least as tall, so the count IS the index.
            queue.Insert(person[1], person);
        }

        return queue.ToArray();
    }

    private static string Show(int[][] q) =>
        q.Length == 0 ? "(empty)" : string.Join(" ", q.Select(p => $"[{p[0]},{p[1]}]"));

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Show(ReconstructQueue(new[] {
            new[] { 7, 0 }, new[] { 4, 4 }, new[] { 7, 1 },
            new[] { 5, 0 }, new[] { 6, 1 }, new[] { 5, 2 } })));

        Console.WriteLine(Show(ReconstructQueue(new[] {
            new[] { 6, 0 }, new[] { 5, 0 }, new[] { 4, 0 },
            new[] { 3, 2 }, new[] { 2, 2 }, new[] { 1, 4 } })));

        Console.WriteLine(Show(ReconstructQueue(new[] { new[] { 1, 0 } })));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(ReconstructQueue(Array.Empty<int[]>())));
    }
}
```

