**Approach**

Sort, then two pointers from both ends. Pair the lightest with the heaviest whenever they fit together; otherwise the heaviest goes alone.

**Logic**

The heaviest person must travel, so the only question is whether anyone can accompany them. If the lightest cannot, nobody can, so they go alone. If the lightest can, pairing them is optimal: the lightest is the easiest to place later, so using them here costs the least future flexibility.

That exchange argument is what makes the greedy provable rather than merely plausible.

O(n log n) for the sort, then one linear pass.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int NumRescueBoats(int[] people, int limit)
    {
        if (people == null || people.Length == 0) return 0;

        Array.Sort(people);

        int light = 0, heavy = people.Length - 1, boats = 0;

        while (light <= heavy)
        {
            // The heaviest always boards. Take the lightest too if they fit.
            if (people[light] + people[heavy] <= limit) light++;

            heavy--;
            boats++;
        }

        return boats;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(NumRescueBoats(new[] { 1, 2 }, 3));
        Console.WriteLine(NumRescueBoats(new[] { 3, 2, 2, 1 }, 3));
        Console.WriteLine(NumRescueBoats(new[] { 3, 5, 3, 4 }, 5));   // nobody pairs
        Console.WriteLine(NumRescueBoats(new[] { 5 }, 5));
        Console.WriteLine(NumRescueBoats(Array.Empty<int>(), 5));
        Console.WriteLine(NumRescueBoats(new[] { 1, 1, 1, 1 }, 2));
    }
}
