**Approach**

Greedy in one pass. Track two running lengths: the longest wiggle ending on a rise, and the longest ending on a fall. Each new element extends one of them.

**Logic**

If the next element is larger, the best sequence ending on a rise becomes one more than the best previously ending on a fall — because the sequence must alternate. If smaller, the mirror applies. If equal, neither changes, which is how plateaus are ignored without a special case.

The answer is the larger of the two counters. O(n) time, O(1) space.

The DP formulation with two arrays is the same recurrence; the rolling variables are just the space-optimised version.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int WiggleMaxLength(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length == 0) return 0;

        int up = 1, down = 1;   // longest ending on a rise / on a fall

        for (int i = 1; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            if (nums[i] > nums[i - 1]) up = down + 1;
            else if (nums[i] < nums[i - 1]) down = up + 1;
            // equal: a plateau extends neither
        }

        return Math.Max(up, down);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(WiggleMaxLength(new[] { 1, 7, 4, 9, 2, 5 }));      // all alternate
        Console.WriteLine(WiggleMaxLength(new[] { 1, 17, 5, 10, 13, 15, 10, 5, 16, 8 }));
        Console.WriteLine(WiggleMaxLength(new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }));         // monotonic
        Console.WriteLine(WiggleMaxLength(new[] { 3, 3, 3, 3 }));            // all equal
        Console.WriteLine(WiggleMaxLength(new[] { 5 }));
        Console.WriteLine(WiggleMaxLength(new[] { 1, 2, 2, 1 }));
    }
}
