**Approach**

Scan for the first place the digits decrease, decrement the digit before it, and set everything after to nine.

**Logic**

When digit i is greater than digit i+1, the number cannot stay as it is. Reducing digit i by one and making everything after it nine gives the largest candidate below that prefix.

The subtlety is that decrementing can create a new violation further left: 332 becomes 3 2 9, but now 3 > 2. So the scan must continue backwards from the point of change, which is why the loop tracks the leftmost position that needs filling with nines.

O(number of digits), which is O(log n).

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int MonotoneIncreasingDigits(int n)
    {
        if (n < 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(n));

        var digits = n.ToString().ToCharArray();
        int fillFrom = digits.Length;   // everything from here becomes '9'

        // Right to left: a decrement can create a new violation further left.
        for (int i = digits.Length - 1; i > 0; i--)
        {
            if (digits[i - 1] <= digits[i]) continue;

            digits[i - 1]--;
            fillFrom = i;
        }

        for (int i = fillFrom; i < digits.Length; i++) digits[i] = '9';

        return int.Parse(new string(digits));
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(MonotoneIncreasingDigits(10));
        Console.WriteLine(MonotoneIncreasingDigits(1234));    // already monotone
        Console.WriteLine(MonotoneIncreasingDigits(332));      // cascading decrement
        Console.WriteLine(MonotoneIncreasingDigits(0));
        Console.WriteLine(MonotoneIncreasingDigits(100));
        Console.WriteLine(MonotoneIncreasingDigits(668841));
    }
}
