```
[9] [3,15] [20] [7]
[4] [2] [1,5,6] [3] [7]
[1]
(empty)
```

**Approach**

A single pre-order traversal carrying the maximum seen on the path so far. A node is good when its value is at least that maximum.

**Logic**

The root is always good, since the path to it contains only itself. Descending, each child receives the larger of the running maximum and the current node's value.

Passing the maximum down rather than computing it on the way up is what keeps this a single pass. The property depends only on ancestors, so it is naturally a pre-order quantity.

O(n) time, O(h) stack space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class TreeNode
{
    public int Val;
    public TreeNode Left, Right;
    public TreeNode(int val = 0, TreeNode left = null, TreeNode right = null)
    { Val = val; Left = left; Right = right; }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static int GoodNodes(TreeNode root) =>
        root == null ? 0 : Count(root, int.MinValue);

    private static int Count(TreeNode node, int maxOnPath)
    {
        if (node == null) return 0;

        // >= because an equal value still has nothing LARGER before it.
        int good = node.Val >= maxOnPath ? 1 : 0;

        int updated = Math.Max(maxOnPath, node.Val);

        return good + Count(node.Left, updated) + Count(node.Right, updated);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        //     3
        //    / \
        //   1   4
        //  /   / \
        // 3   1   5
        Console.WriteLine(GoodNodes(new TreeNode(3,
            new TreeNode(1, new TreeNode(3)),
            new TreeNode(4, new TreeNode(1), new TreeNode(5)))));

        //   3
        //  /
        // 3
        //  \
        //   4
        Console.WriteLine(GoodNodes(new TreeNode(3,
            new TreeNode(3, null, new TreeNode(4)))));

        Console.WriteLine(GoodNodes(new TreeNode(1)));
        Console.WriteLine(GoodNodes(null));

        // Strictly decreasing: only the root is good.
        Console.WriteLine(GoodNodes(new TreeNode(5, new TreeNode(4, new TreeNode(3)))));

        // Negative values still work, which int.MinValue as the seed guarantees.
        Console.WriteLine(GoodNodes(new TreeNode(-5, new TreeNode(-3))));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
4
3
1
0
1
2
```

**Approach**

For every node of the larger tree whose value matches the candidate root, run an identical-trees check from there. O(n·m) worst case.

The faster alternative serialises both trees with explicit null markers and asks whether one string contains the other, which with a linear substring search is O(n + m).

**Logic**

The identical check must compare structure and values together: both null is a match, exactly one null is not.

For the serialisation approach, the null markers are essential. Without them the serialisation of a node with one child is ambiguous, so a subtree could be "found" that is not actually present — the classic false positive being a tree whose values appear in the right order but in a different shape.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Text;

public class TreeNode
{
    public int Val;
    public TreeNode Left, Right;
    public TreeNode(int val = 0, TreeNode left = null, TreeNode right = null)
    { Val = val; Left = left; Right = right; }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static bool IsSubtree(TreeNode root, TreeNode sub)
    {
        if (sub == null) return true;
        if (root == null) return false;

        return IsSame(root, sub)
            || IsSubtree(root.Left, sub)
            || IsSubtree(root.Right, sub);
    }

    private static bool IsSame(TreeNode a, TreeNode b)
    {
        if (a == null && b == null) return true;
        if (a == null || b == null) return false;

        return a.Val == b.Val && IsSame(a.Left, b.Left) && IsSame(a.Right, b.Right);
    }

    // O(n + m): serialise with null markers, then substring search.
    public static bool IsSubtreeBySerialisation(TreeNode root, TreeNode sub) =>
        Serialise(root).Contains(Serialise(sub), StringComparison.Ordinal);

    private static string Serialise(TreeNode node)
    {
        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        Write(node, sb);
        return sb.ToString();
    }

    private static void Write(TreeNode node, StringBuilder sb)
    {
        // The leading marker prevents 2 matching inside 12.
        if (node == null) { sb.Append(",#"); return; }

        sb.Append(',').Append(node.Val);
        Write(node.Left, sb);
        Write(node.Right, sb);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        //     3
        //    / \
        //   4   5
        //  / \
        // 1   2
        var root = new TreeNode(3,
            new TreeNode(4, new TreeNode(1), new TreeNode(2)),
            new TreeNode(5));

        var sub = new TreeNode(4, new TreeNode(1), new TreeNode(2));
        Console.WriteLine(IsSubtree(root, sub));
        Console.WriteLine(IsSubtreeBySerialisation(root, sub));

        // Same values but the subtree has an extra node, so it is NOT present.
        var deeper = new TreeNode(4, new TreeNode(1), new TreeNode(2, new TreeNode(0)));
        Console.WriteLine(IsSubtree(root, deeper));

        Console.WriteLine(IsSubtree(root, new TreeNode(5)));
        Console.WriteLine(IsSubtree(null, new TreeNode(1)));
        Console.WriteLine(IsSubtree(root, null));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
True
True
False
True
False
True
```

**Approach**

An in-order traversal of a valid BST is strictly increasing. Swapping two nodes creates one or two places where that order breaks, and identifying them locates the culprits.

**Logic**

Walk in order tracking the previous node. When `previous.Val > current.Val`, an inversion has been found.

If the swapped nodes are **not adjacent** in order, there are two inversions: the first node is the earlier element of the first inversion, the second is the later element of the second. If they **are adjacent**, there is only one inversion and both culprits come from it.

Handling both cases with the same code is the trick: record the first culprit only on the first inversion, and the second culprit on every inversion, so a single inversion sets both.

O(n) time, O(h) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class TreeNode
{
    public int Val;
    public TreeNode Left, Right;
    public TreeNode(int val = 0, TreeNode left = null, TreeNode right = null)
    { Val = val; Left = left; Right = right; }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void RecoverTree(TreeNode root)
    {
        TreeNode first = null, second = null, previous = null;

        // Iterative in-order: no recursion depth limit.
        var stack = new Stack<TreeNode>();
        var node = root;

        while (node != null || stack.Count > 0)
        {
            while (node != null) { stack.Push(node); node = node.Left; }
            node = stack.Pop();

            if (previous != null && previous.Val > node.Val)
            {
                first ??= previous;   // only the FIRST inversion sets this
                second = node;        // every inversion updates this
            }

            previous = node;
            node = node.Right;
        }

        if (first != null) (first.Val, second.Val) = (second.Val, first.Val);
    }

    private static string InOrder(TreeNode node)
    {
        var values = new List<int>();
        void Walk(TreeNode n) { if (n == null) return; Walk(n.Left); values.Add(n.Val); Walk(n.Right); }
        Walk(node);
        return values.Count == 0 ? "(empty)" : string.Join(",", values);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        // In-order reads 3,2,1 and must become 1,2,3.
        //   3            1
        //  / \    ->    / \
        // 1   2        3   2   (in-order 3,1,2 should be 1,2,3)
        var a = new TreeNode(1, new TreeNode(3, null, new TreeNode(2)));
        Console.WriteLine(InOrder(a));
        RecoverTree(a);
        Console.WriteLine(InOrder(a));

        // Adjacent swap: a single inversion.
        var b = new TreeNode(3, new TreeNode(1), new TreeNode(4, new TreeNode(2)));
        Console.WriteLine(InOrder(b));
        RecoverTree(b);
        Console.WriteLine(InOrder(b));

        // Already valid: nothing changes.
        var c = new TreeNode(2, new TreeNode(1), new TreeNode(3));
        RecoverTree(c);
        Console.WriteLine(InOrder(c));

        var single = new TreeNode(1);
        RecoverTree(single);
        Console.WriteLine(InOrder(single));
    }
}
