400
500
200
-1
0
```

**Approach**

Union-find, processing edges in order. The first edge whose endpoints are already connected is the one closing a cycle, and the problem asks for the last such edge in input order — which, since exactly one cycle exists, is the first one found.

**Logic**

A tree on n nodes has n-1 edges. Given n edges, exactly one creates a cycle. Union-find detects it: if `Find` returns the same root for both endpoints, they were already connected and this edge is redundant.

Path compression and union by rank keep each operation effectively constant.

O(n α(n)), which is linear for practical purposes.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int[] FindRedundantConnection(int[][] edges)
    {
        int n = edges.Length;
        var parent = new int[n + 1];
        var rank = new int[n + 1];
        for (int i = 0; i <= n; i++) parent[i] = i;

        int Find(int x)
        {
            while (parent[x] != x) { parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]; x = parent[x]; }
            return x;
        }

        foreach (var edge in edges)
        {
            int a = Find(edge[0]), b = Find(edge[1]);

            // Already connected: this edge closes the cycle.
            if (a == b) return edge;

            if (rank[a] < rank[b]) (a, b) = (b, a);
            parent[b] = a;
            if (rank[a] == rank[b]) rank[a]++;
        }

        return Array.Empty<int>();
    }

    private static string Show(int[] e) => e.Length == 0 ? "(none)" : $"[{e[0]},{e[1]}]";

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Show(FindRedundantConnection(new[] {
            new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 1, 3 }, new[] { 2, 3 } })));

        Console.WriteLine(Show(FindRedundantConnection(new[] {
            new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 2, 3 }, new[] { 3, 4 },
            new[] { 1, 4 }, new[] { 1, 5 } })));

        Console.WriteLine(Show(FindRedundantConnection(new[] {
            new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 1, 2 } })));            // a parallel edge

        Console.WriteLine(Show(FindRedundantConnection(new[] {
            new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 2, 3 }, new[] { 1, 3 } })));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
[2,3]
[1,4]
[1,2]
[1,3]
```

**Approach**

Union-find over emails. Every email in one account belongs to the same person, so union them all with the first. Accounts sharing any email end up in the same component.

**Logic**

Union the emails rather than the accounts, because the shared email is the evidence of identity and an account may link two previously separate groups.

After unioning, group emails by their root, sort each group, and attach the owner's name — which is looked up from any email in the group, since all emails in a component belong to one person.

Sorting is required by the problem and also makes the output deterministic.

O(n log n) dominated by the sort.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution
{
    public static IList<IList<string>> AccountsMerge(IList<IList<string>> accounts)
    {
        var parent = new Dictionary<string, string>();
        var owner = new Dictionary<string, string>();

        string Find(string x)
        {
            while (parent[x] != x) { parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]; x = parent[x]; }
            return x;
        }

        foreach (var account in accounts)
        {
            string name = account[0];

            for (int i = 1; i < account.Count; i++)
            {
                string email = account[i];
                parent.TryAdd(email, email);
                owner[email] = name;

                // Every email in one account is the same person.
                if (i > 1) parent[Find(email)] = Find(account[1]);
            }
        }

        var groups = new Dictionary<string, List<string>>();

        foreach (string email in parent.Keys)
        {
            string root = Find(email);
            if (!groups.TryGetValue(root, out var list)) groups[root] = list = new List<string>();
            list.Add(email);
        }

        return groups.Select(g =>
        {
            var emails = g.Value;
            emails.Sort(StringComparer.Ordinal);
            var row = new List<string> { owner[g.Key] };
            row.AddRange(emails);
            return (IList<string>)row;
        }).ToList();
    }

    private static string Show(IList<IList<string>> r)
    {
        var rendered = r.Select(g => "[" + string.Join(",", g) + "]").ToList();
        rendered.Sort(StringComparer.Ordinal);
        return rendered.Count == 0 ? "(none)" : string.Join(" ", rendered);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        var accounts = new List<IList<string>>
        {
            new List<string> { "John", "a@x.com", "b@x.com" },
            new List<string> { "John", "b@x.com", "c@x.com" },   // shares b, so merges
            new List<string> { "Mary", "m@x.com" },
            new List<string> { "John", "z@x.com" },              // a different John
        };

        Console.WriteLine(Show(AccountsMerge(accounts)));

        Console.WriteLine(Show(AccountsMerge(new List<IList<string>>
        {
            new List<string> { "A", "one@x.com" },
        })));

        Console.WriteLine(Show(AccountsMerge(new List<IList<string>>())));
    }
}
```

