0.5
-1
1
-1
0.16667
```

**Approach**

Peel leaves layer by layer. The nodes remaining at the end are the centroids, and there are at most two of them.

**Logic**

Rooting at a leaf always gives a tall tree, so the optimal roots are as far from the leaves as possible — the centre of the graph. Repeatedly removing all current leaves shrinks the tree inward, and the last one or two nodes standing are the centres.

There are at most two because a tree's centre is either one node, when the longest path has even length, or two adjacent nodes, when it is odd. Stopping when two or fewer remain is therefore correct.

O(n) time and space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution
{
    public static IList<int> FindMinHeightTrees(int n, int[][] edges)
    {
        if (n <= 2) return Enumerable.Range(0, n).ToList();

        var adjacency = new List<int>[n];
        var degree = new int[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) adjacency[i] = new List<int>();

        foreach (var e in edges)
        {
            adjacency[e[0]].Add(e[1]);
            adjacency[e[1]].Add(e[0]);
            degree[e[0]]++;
            degree[e[1]]++;
        }

        var leaves = new List<int>();
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) if (degree[i] == 1) leaves.Add(i);

        int remaining = n;

        // Peel inward until at most two nodes are left: those are the centres.
        while (remaining > 2)
        {
            remaining -= leaves.Count;
            var next = new List<int>();

            foreach (int leaf in leaves)
                foreach (int neighbour in adjacency[leaf])
                    if (--degree[neighbour] == 1) next.Add(neighbour);

            leaves = next;
        }

        return leaves;
    }

    private static string Show(IList<int> r)
    {
        var copy = new List<int>(r);
        copy.Sort();
        return copy.Count == 0 ? "(none)" : string.Join(",", copy);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Show(FindMinHeightTrees(4, new[] {
            new[] { 1, 0 }, new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 1, 3 } })));      // a star: centre is 1

        Console.WriteLine(Show(FindMinHeightTrees(6, new[] {
            new[] { 3, 0 }, new[] { 3, 1 }, new[] { 3, 2 },
            new[] { 3, 4 }, new[] { 5, 4 } })));

        Console.WriteLine(Show(FindMinHeightTrees(2, new[] { new[] { 0, 1 } })));  // two centres
        Console.WriteLine(Show(FindMinHeightTrees(1, Array.Empty<int[]>())));
        Console.WriteLine(Show(FindMinHeightTrees(4, new[] {
            new[] { 0, 1 }, new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 2, 3 } })));      // a path: two centres
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
1
3,4
0,1
0
1,2
```

**Approach**

Hierholzer's algorithm for an Eulerian path. Every ticket must be used exactly once, which is precisely an Eulerian path over the directed graph of flights.

**Logic**

A naive greedy — always take the smallest available destination — can strand you at a dead end while tickets remain. Hierholzer's fixes that: walk until stuck, then add the stuck airport to the front of the route and back up. The route is built in reverse.

Using a min-heap or sorted list per airport gives the lexicographically smallest itinerary, which the problem requires.

O(E log E) for the sorting.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Linq;

public class Solution
{
    public static IList<string> FindItinerary(IList<IList<string>> tickets)
    {
        var destinations = new Dictionary<string, PriorityQueue<string, string>>();

        foreach (var t in tickets)
        {
            if (!destinations.TryGetValue(t[0], out var queue))
                destinations[t[0]] = queue = new PriorityQueue<string, string>();

            queue.Enqueue(t[1], t[1]);   // lexicographic order
        }

        var route = new List<string>();
        var stack = new Stack<string>();
        stack.Push("JFK");

        while (stack.Count > 0)
        {
            string airport = stack.Peek();

            // Walk as far as possible; when stuck, this airport joins the route.
            if (destinations.TryGetValue(airport, out var queue) && queue.Count > 0)
                stack.Push(queue.Dequeue());
            else
                route.Add(stack.Pop());
        }

        route.Reverse();   // built backwards
        return route;
    }

    private static IList<IList<string>> Tickets(params string[][] pairs) =>
        pairs.Select(p => (IList<string>)new List<string> { p[0], p[1] }).ToList();

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join("->", FindItinerary(Tickets(
            new[] { "MUC", "LHR" }, new[] { "JFK", "MUC" },
            new[] { "SFO", "SJC" }, new[] { "LHR", "SFO" }))));

        // The greedy trap: taking ATL first from JFK strands you.
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join("->", FindItinerary(Tickets(
            new[] { "JFK", "SFO" }, new[] { "JFK", "ATL" },
            new[] { "SFO", "ATL" }, new[] { "ATL", "JFK" },
            new[] { "ATL", "SFO" }))));

        Console.WriteLine(string.Join("->", FindItinerary(Tickets(
            new[] { "JFK", "KUL" }, new[] { "JFK", "NRT" }, new[] { "NRT", "JFK" }))));

        Console.WriteLine(string.Join("->", FindItinerary(Tickets(
            new[] { "JFK", "AAA" }))));
    }
}
```

