[1,3]
(none)
[0,1] [1,2]
[0,1]
(none)
```

**Approach**

Multi-source BFS seeded with every gate at once. The first time a room is reached is by the shortest path from the nearest gate.

**Logic**

Running a separate BFS from each gate and taking minimums would be correct but far slower. Seeding the queue with all gates makes the initial frontier distance zero everywhere a gate exists, so BFS naturally computes the minimum over all of them in one traversal.

Rooms are marked as they are enqueued, using the fact that an unvisited room holds the sentinel value. Walls are skipped and never enqueued.

O(rows × cols) time.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    private const int Wall = -1;
    private const int Gate = 0;
    private const int Empty = int.MaxValue;

    public static void WallsAndGates(int[][] rooms)
    {
        if (rooms == null || rooms.Length == 0) return;

        int height = rooms.Length, width = rooms[0].Length;
        var queue = new Queue<(int r, int c)>();

        // Every gate is a source, so one BFS computes the minimum over all of them.
        for (int r = 0; r < height; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < width; c++)
                if (rooms[r][c] == Gate) queue.Enqueue((r, c));

        int[] dr = { -1, 1, 0, 0 };
        int[] dc = { 0, 0, -1, 1 };

        while (queue.Count > 0)
        {
            var (r, c) = queue.Dequeue();

            for (int d = 0; d < 4; d++)
            {
                int nr = r + dr[d], nc = c + dc[d];

                if (nr < 0 || nr >= height || nc < 0 || nc >= width) continue;
                if (rooms[nr][nc] != Empty) continue;   // wall, gate, or already reached

                rooms[nr][nc] = rooms[r][c] + 1;
                queue.Enqueue((nr, nc));
            }
        }
    }

    private static string Render(int[][] m)
    {
        var rows = new List<string>();
        foreach (var row in m)
        {
            var cells = new List<string>();
            foreach (int v in row) cells.Add(v == Empty ? "INF" : v.ToString());
            rows.Add(string.Join(",", cells));
        }
        return string.Join(" / ", rows);
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        var rooms = new[] {
            new[] { Empty, Wall, Gate, Empty },
            new[] { Empty, Empty, Empty, Wall },
            new[] { Empty, Wall, Empty, Wall },
            new[] { Gate, Wall, Empty, Empty } };

        WallsAndGates(rooms);
        Console.WriteLine(Render(rooms));

        // A room walled off from every gate stays at INF.
        var isolated = new[] {
            new[] { Gate, Wall, Empty } };
        WallsAndGates(isolated);
        Console.WriteLine(Render(isolated));

        var noGates = new[] { new[] { Empty, Empty } };
        WallsAndGates(noGates);
        Console.WriteLine(Render(noGates));

        var onlyGate = new[] { new[] { Gate } };
        WallsAndGates(onlyGate);
        Console.WriteLine(Render(onlyGate));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
3,-1,0,1 / 2,2,1,-1 / 1,-1,2,-1 / 0,-1,3,4
0,-1,INF
INF,INF
0
```

**Approach**

BFS over combinations. Each state is a four-digit string; each move turns one wheel one notch, giving eight neighbours. Deadends are simply pre-marked as visited so they are never entered.

**Logic**

Every turn costs the same, so BFS gives the minimum number of turns. The state space is only 10,000 combinations, so exhaustive search is cheap.

Two cases must be checked before the search: the start itself may be a deadend, in which case there is no solution, and the target may equal the start, which costs zero turns.

Wheels wrap, so 0 turns down to 9 and 9 turns up to 0. That wraparound uses the non-negative modulo idiom.

O(10000 × 8) time.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int OpenLock(string[] deadends, string target)
    {
        var blocked = new HashSet<string>(deadends);

        if (blocked.Contains("0000")) return -1;   // cannot even start
        if (target == "0000") return 0;

        var visited = new HashSet<string>(blocked) { "0000" };
        var queue = new Queue<string>();
        queue.Enqueue("0000");

        int turns = 0;

        while (queue.Count > 0)
        {
            turns++;
            int levelSize = queue.Count;

            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; i++)
            {
                string state = queue.Dequeue();

                foreach (string next in Neighbours(state))
                {
                    if (!visited.Add(next)) continue;   // blocked or already seen

                    if (next == target) return turns;
                    queue.Enqueue(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return -1;
    }

    private static IEnumerable<string> Neighbours(string state)
    {
        var wheels = state.ToCharArray();

        for (int i = 0; i < 4; i++)
        {
            char original = wheels[i];
            int digit = original - '0';

            // Wheels wrap: 9 up is 0, 0 down is 9.
            wheels[i] = (char)('0' + (digit + 1) % 10);
            yield return new string(wheels);

            wheels[i] = (char)('0' + (digit + 9) % 10);
            yield return new string(wheels);

            wheels[i] = original;
        }
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(OpenLock(new[] { "0201", "0101", "0102", "1212", "2002" }, "0202"));
        Console.WriteLine(OpenLock(new[] { "8888" }, "0009"));          // one turn down
        Console.WriteLine(OpenLock(new[] { "8887", "8889", "8878", "8898",
                                           "8788", "8988", "7888", "9888" }, "8888"));
        Console.WriteLine(OpenLock(new[] { "0000" }, "8888"));          // start blocked
        Console.WriteLine(OpenLock(Array.Empty<string>(), "0000"));     // already there
        Console.WriteLine(OpenLock(Array.Empty<string>(), "1111"));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
6
1
-1
-1
0
4
```

**Approach**

One traversal per unvisited city; each traversal consumes exactly one province. Either DFS or union-find works, and the adjacency matrix makes neighbours a row scan.

**Logic**

Start at city 0; mark everything reachable. That is one province. Move to the next unmarked city and repeat. The number of traversals started is the number of provinces.

The matrix is symmetric and the diagonal is always 1, since a city is connected to itself, so the self-edge needs no special handling — it simply revisits an already-marked city.

O(n²) because the matrix itself is that size.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int FindCircleNum(int[][] connected)
    {
        if (connected == null || connected.Length == 0) return 0;

        int n = connected.Length;
        var visited = new bool[n];
        int provinces = 0;

        for (int start = 0; start < n; start++)
        {
            if (visited[start]) continue;

            provinces++;   // each new traversal consumes exactly one province

            var stack = new Stack<int>();
            stack.Push(start);
            visited[start] = true;

            while (stack.Count > 0)
            {
                int city = stack.Pop();

                for (int next = 0; next < n; next++)
                {
                    if (connected[city][next] != 1 || visited[next]) continue;

                    visited[next] = true;
                    stack.Push(next);
                }
            }
        }

        return provinces;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(FindCircleNum(new[] {
            new[] { 1, 1, 0 }, new[] { 1, 1, 0 }, new[] { 0, 0, 1 } }));   // {0,1} and {2}

        Console.WriteLine(FindCircleNum(new[] {
            new[] { 1, 0, 0 }, new[] { 0, 1, 0 }, new[] { 0, 0, 1 } }));   // all isolated

        Console.WriteLine(FindCircleNum(new[] {
            new[] { 1, 1, 1 }, new[] { 1, 1, 1 }, new[] { 1, 1, 1 } }));   // all one

        Console.WriteLine(FindCircleNum(new[] { new[] { 1 } }));
        Console.WriteLine(FindCircleNum(Array.Empty<int[]>()));

        Console.WriteLine(FindCircleNum(new[] {
            new[] { 1, 1, 0, 0 }, new[] { 1, 1, 0, 0 },
            new[] { 0, 0, 1, 1 }, new[] { 0, 0, 1, 1 } }));                // two pairs
    }
}
```

