```
2
3
1
1
0
2
```

**Approach**

Two passes with union-find. Process every equality first, merging variables into components, then check every inequality against the resulting components.

**Logic**

The order is what makes it work. Equalities are constraints that must all hold simultaneously, so applying them all first produces the complete partition of variables into groups that must be equal. Only then can an inequality be evaluated: it is violated exactly when both sides ended up in the same component.

Processing them interleaved would evaluate an inequality before a later equality merges the two sides, missing the contradiction.

O(n α(n)) for 26 variables.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool EquationsPossible(string[] equations)
    {
        var parent = new int[26];
        for (int i = 0; i < 26; i++) parent[i] = i;

        int Find(int x)
        {
            while (parent[x] != x) { parent[x] = parent[parent[x]]; x = parent[x]; }
            return x;
        }

        // Pass 1: every equality, so the partition is complete before checking.
        foreach (string e in equations)
            if (e[1] == '=')
                parent[Find(e[0] - 'a')] = Find(e[3] - 'a');

        // Pass 2: an inequality is violated if both sides are in one component.
        foreach (string e in equations)
            if (e[1] == '!' && Find(e[0] - 'a') == Find(e[3] - 'a'))
                return false;

        return true;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(EquationsPossible(new[] { "a==b", "b!=a" }));
        Console.WriteLine(EquationsPossible(new[] { "a==b", "b==c", "a==c" }));
        Console.WriteLine(EquationsPossible(new[] { "a==b", "b==c", "a!=c" }));   // transitive
        Console.WriteLine(EquationsPossible(new[] { "a!=a" }));                    // self
        Console.WriteLine(EquationsPossible(new[] { "c==c", "b==d", "x!=z" }));

        // The inequality appears BEFORE the equality that contradicts it.
        Console.WriteLine(EquationsPossible(new[] { "a!=c", "b==c", "a==b" }));
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
False
True
False
False
True
False
```

**Approach**

Dijkstra with the comparison inverted. Probabilities multiply along a path and we want the maximum, so the priority queue must pop the highest probability rather than the lowest cost.

**Logic**

The key property that makes Dijkstra valid is that extending a path cannot improve it. With additive non-negative weights, adding an edge increases the cost. With probabilities in [0,1], multiplying by another edge decreases the product. Both are monotonic in the required direction, so settling a node is safe.

.NET's PriorityQueue is a min-heap, so negate the probability to get max-heap behaviour.

O((V + E) log V).

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static double MaxProbability(int n, int[][] edges, double[] probabilities,
                                        int start, int end)
    {
        var graph = new List<(int To, double P)>[n];
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) graph[i] = new List<(int, double)>();

        for (int i = 0; i < edges.Length; i++)
        {
            graph[edges[i][0]].Add((edges[i][1], probabilities[i]));
            graph[edges[i][1]].Add((edges[i][0], probabilities[i]));
        }

        var best = new double[n];
        best[start] = 1.0;

        var settled = new bool[n];

        // Min-heap, so negate to pop the HIGHEST probability first.
        var heap = new PriorityQueue<int, double>();
        heap.Enqueue(start, -1.0);

        while (heap.Count > 0)
        {
            int node = heap.Dequeue();
            if (settled[node]) continue;
            settled[node] = true;

            if (node == end) return best[node];

            foreach (var (next, p) in graph[node])
            {
                double candidate = best[node] * p;

                if (candidate > best[next])
                {
                    best[next] = candidate;
                    heap.Enqueue(next, -candidate);
                }
            }
        }

        return 0.0;   // unreachable
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Math.Round(MaxProbability(3,
            new[] { new[] { 0, 1 }, new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 0, 2 } },
            new[] { 0.5, 0.5, 0.2 }, 0, 2), 5));

        Console.WriteLine(Math.Round(MaxProbability(3,
            new[] { new[] { 0, 1 }, new[] { 1, 2 }, new[] { 0, 2 } },
            new[] { 0.5, 0.5, 0.3 }, 0, 2), 5));

        Console.WriteLine(MaxProbability(3,
            new[] { new[] { 0, 1 } }, new[] { 0.5 }, 0, 2));       // unreachable

        Console.WriteLine(MaxProbability(2, Array.Empty<int[]>(), Array.Empty<double>(), 0, 0));
        Console.WriteLine(Math.Round(MaxProbability(2,
            new[] { new[] { 0, 1 } }, new[] { 0.7 }, 0, 1), 5));
    }
}
```

