6
0
5
0
4
```

**Approach**

Reduce it to house robber. Taking a value earns its total contribution and forbids the adjacent values, which is exactly the no-two-adjacent constraint over the value axis rather than the array axis.

**Logic**

First aggregate: the points for value v are v times how many times it appears, because taking v takes all copies at once. Then walk the value range in order, and at each value choose between taking it plus the best from two values back, or skipping it.

The reduction is the whole insight. Working on the original array directly is much harder, because the constraint is about values rather than positions.

O(n + maxValue) time.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int DeleteAndEarn(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length == 0) return 0;

        int max = 0;
        foreach (int n in nums) max = Math.Max(max, n);

        // points[v] = total earned by taking value v, which takes every copy.
        var points = new int[max + 1];
        foreach (int n in nums) points[n] += n;

        // Now it is house robber over the value axis.
        int twoBack = 0, oneBack = 0;

        for (int value = 1; value <= max; value++)
        {
            int current = Math.Max(oneBack, twoBack + points[value]);
            twoBack = oneBack;
            oneBack = current;
        }

        return oneBack;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(DeleteAndEarn(new[] { 3, 4, 2 }));
        Console.WriteLine(DeleteAndEarn(new[] { 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4 }));
        Console.WriteLine(DeleteAndEarn(new[] { 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(DeleteAndEarn(new[] { 1, 1, 1, 1 }));        // all the same value
        Console.WriteLine(DeleteAndEarn(new[] { 2, 2, 3, 3, 3, 4, 9 })); // a gap frees 9
        Console.WriteLine(DeleteAndEarn(Array.Empty<int>()));
    }
}
```

