**Approach**

The same recurrence as maximal square, but summing rather than maximising. Each cell's value becomes the side length of the largest square ending there, and that number is also the count of squares ending at that cell.

**Logic**

A cell holding 1 can end squares of side 1, 2, up to its computed value — and exactly that many. So summing every cell's value counts every square in the matrix, each exactly once, because a square is identified uniquely by its bottom-right corner and its side.

The recurrence is one plus the minimum of the three neighbours, since all three must support the square.

O(rows × cols) time, O(1) extra space when modifying in place.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int CountSquares(int[][] matrix)
    {
        if (matrix == null || matrix.Length == 0) return 0;

        int rows = matrix.Length, cols = matrix[0].Length, total = 0;

        for (int r = 0; r < rows; r++)
            for (int c = 0; c < cols; c++)
            {
                if (matrix[r][c] == 0) continue;

                // Interior cells extend the smallest supporting neighbour.
                if (r > 0 && c > 0)
                    matrix[r][c] = 1 + Math.Min(matrix[r - 1][c],
                                      Math.Min(matrix[r][c - 1], matrix[r - 1][c - 1]));

                // The value IS the number of squares ending at this cell.
                total += matrix[r][c];
            }

        return total;
    }

    private static int[][] Grid(params int[][] rows) => rows;

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(CountSquares(Grid(
            new[] { 0, 1, 1, 1 }, new[] { 1, 1, 1, 1 }, new[] { 0, 1, 1, 1 })));

        Console.WriteLine(CountSquares(Grid(
            new[] { 1, 0, 1 }, new[] { 1, 1, 0 }, new[] { 1, 1, 0 })));

        Console.WriteLine(CountSquares(Grid(new[] { 1, 1 }, new[] { 1, 1 })));   // 4 + 1
        Console.WriteLine(CountSquares(Grid(new[] { 0, 0 }, new[] { 0, 0 })));
        Console.WriteLine(CountSquares(Grid(new[] { 1 })));
        Console.WriteLine(CountSquares(Grid(new[] { 1, 1, 1 })));                // single row
    }
}
```

