**Approach**

Bottom-up DP. Working from the penultimate row upward, each cell becomes its own value plus the cheaper of the two cells below it. The apex then holds the answer.

**Logic**

Going bottom-up avoids the edge cases that plague the top-down direction. From the top, a cell's predecessors may be one or two depending on position and require bounds checks; from the bottom, every cell has exactly two children because the row below is always one longer.

The answer ends up in a single cell rather than needing a scan, which is the other advantage.

O(n²) time, O(n) space using one rolling row.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int MinimumTotal(IList<IList<int>> triangle)
    {
        if (triangle == null || triangle.Count == 0) return 0;

        // Seed with the bottom row.
        var best = new int[triangle.Count + 1];

        for (int row = triangle.Count - 1; row >= 0; row--)
            for (int col = 0; col <= row; col++)
                // Every cell has exactly two children going upward: no bounds checks.
                best[col] = triangle[row][col] + Math.Min(best[col], best[col + 1]);

        return best[0];
    }

    private static IList<IList<int>> Triangle(params int[][] rows)
    {
        var t = new List<IList<int>>();
        foreach (var r in rows) t.Add(new List<int>(r));
        return t;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(MinimumTotal(Triangle(
            new[] { 2 }, new[] { 3, 4 }, new[] { 6, 5, 7 }, new[] { 4, 1, 8, 3 })));

        Console.WriteLine(MinimumTotal(Triangle(new[] { -10 })));

        Console.WriteLine(MinimumTotal(Triangle(
            new[] { 1 }, new[] { 2, 3 })));

        Console.WriteLine(MinimumTotal(Triangle(
            new[] { -1 }, new[] { 2, 3 }, new[] { 1, -1, -3 })));

        Console.WriteLine(MinimumTotal(Triangle()));
    }
}
```

