6
0
1
6
10
```

**Approach**

Sort by width ascending, and by height *descending* within equal widths. Then the answer is the longest strictly increasing subsequence of the heights, computed in O(n log n) with patience sorting.

**Logic**

The descending height tie-break is the entire trick. Envelopes of equal width can never nest in each other, so they must never both be picked. Sorting their heights descending makes them a decreasing run, and a strictly increasing subsequence can take at most one element from a decreasing run — the constraint enforces itself.

With ascending heights instead, two envelopes of the same width would look nestable and the answer comes out too large.

O(n log n).

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int MaxEnvelopes(int[][] envelopes)
    {
        if (envelopes == null || envelopes.Length == 0) return 0;

        // Width ascending; height DESCENDING within equal widths so that
        // same-width envelopes form a decreasing run and at most one is taken.
        Array.Sort(envelopes, (a, b) =>
            a[0] != b[0] ? a[0].CompareTo(b[0]) : b[1].CompareTo(a[1]));

        var tails = new List<int>();   // tails[i] = smallest tail of an LIS of length i+1

        foreach (var envelope in envelopes)
        {
            int height = envelope[1];

            int lo = 0, hi = tails.Count;
            while (lo < hi)
            {
                int mid = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;
                if (tails[mid] < height) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid;
            }

            if (lo == tails.Count) tails.Add(height);
            else tails[lo] = height;
        }

        return tails.Count;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(MaxEnvelopes(new[] {
            new[] { 5, 4 }, new[] { 6, 4 }, new[] { 6, 7 }, new[] { 2, 3 } }));

        Console.WriteLine(MaxEnvelopes(new[] { new[] { 1, 1 }, new[] { 1, 1 }, new[] { 1, 1 } }));

        Console.WriteLine(MaxEnvelopes(new[] {
            new[] { 4, 5 }, new[] { 4, 6 }, new[] { 6, 7 }, new[] { 2, 3 }, new[] { 1, 1 } }));

        Console.WriteLine(MaxEnvelopes(new[] { new[] { 1, 1 } }));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxEnvelopes(Array.Empty<int[]>()));

        // Same width, ascending heights would wrongly report 2 here.
        Console.WriteLine(MaxEnvelopes(new[] { new[] { 3, 1 }, new[] { 3, 9 } }));
    }
}
```

