**Approach**

The answer is the length minus the longest palindromic subsequence — and that subsequence is the longest common subsequence of the string with its reverse.

**Logic**

Every character not in the longest palindromic subsequence needs exactly one insertion to give it a partner. Characters already in it are paired. So the insertions needed is n minus that length.

The reduction to LCS against the reverse works because a palindromic subsequence reads the same forwards and backwards, so it appears in both the string and its reverse.

O(n²) time, O(n) space with a rolling row.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int MinInsertions(string s)
    {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s)) return 0;

        int n = s.Length;
        var reversed = new string(Reverse(s));

        // Longest common subsequence of s and its reverse = longest palindromic subsequence.
        var previous = new int[n + 1];
        var current = new int[n + 1];

        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++)
        {
            for (int j = 1; j <= n; j++)
                current[j] = s[i - 1] == reversed[j - 1]
                    ? previous[j - 1] + 1
                    : Math.Max(previous[j], current[j - 1]);

            (previous, current) = (current, previous);
        }

        return n - previous[n];   // unpaired characters each need one insertion
    }

    private static char[] Reverse(string s)
    {
        var c = s.ToCharArray();
        Array.Reverse(c);
        return c;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(MinInsertions("zzazz"));      // already a palindrome
        Console.WriteLine(MinInsertions("mbadm"));
        Console.WriteLine(MinInsertions("leetcode"));
        Console.WriteLine(MinInsertions(""));
        Console.WriteLine(MinInsertions("a"));
        Console.WriteLine(MinInsertions("abcd"));       // nothing pairs
    }
}
```

