**Approach**

DP carrying one running total per colour. The cost of painting a house a colour is that colour's cost plus the cheapest total ending in a *different* colour on the previous house.

**Logic**

The state cannot be a single number, because the cheapest total so far may end in the very colour you now want to use. Keeping one total per colour is what makes the adjacency constraint checkable.

With three colours the "minimum of the other two" is written out directly. For k colours you would track the best and second-best totals instead, which keeps it linear rather than O(n·k²).

O(n) time, O(1) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int MinCost(int[][] costs)
    {
        if (costs == null || costs.Length == 0) return 0;

        // Cheapest total for all houses so far, ending in each colour.
        int red = costs[0][0], blue = costs[0][1], green = costs[0][2];

        for (int house = 1; house < costs.Length; house++)
        {
            int nextRed   = costs[house][0] + Math.Min(blue, green);
            int nextBlue  = costs[house][1] + Math.Min(red, green);
            int nextGreen = costs[house][2] + Math.Min(red, blue);

            red = nextRed; blue = nextBlue; green = nextGreen;
        }

        return Math.Min(red, Math.Min(blue, green));
    }

    private static int[][] Costs(params int[][] rows) => rows;

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(MinCost(Costs(
            new[] { 17, 2, 17 }, new[] { 16, 16, 5 }, new[] { 14, 3, 19 })));

        Console.WriteLine(MinCost(Costs(new[] { 7, 6, 2 })));
        Console.WriteLine(MinCost(Costs()));

        Console.WriteLine(MinCost(Costs(new[] { 1, 100, 100 }, new[] { 1, 100, 100 })));

        Console.WriteLine(MinCost(Costs(
            new[] { 5, 8, 6 }, new[] { 19, 14, 13 }, new[] { 7, 5, 12 }, new[] { 14, 15, 17 })));
    }
}
