**Approach**

Binary search on the answer. Guess a maximum allowed subarray sum, then greedily check whether k or fewer subarrays suffice under that cap.

**Logic**

The feasibility check is monotonic: if a cap works, every larger cap works too. That monotonicity is what makes binary search valid — you are searching over answers, not over the array.

The search range is bounded below by the largest single element, since no split can make a subarray smaller than its biggest member, and above by the total sum, which is the one-subarray case.

The greedy check is optimal: extending the current subarray until it would exceed the cap always leaves the fewest pieces.

O(n log(sum)).

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int SplitArray(int[] nums, int k)
    {
        long lo = 0, hi = 0;

        foreach (int n in nums) { lo = Math.Max(lo, n); hi += n; }

        // lo: no subarray can be smaller than the largest element.
        // hi: one subarray holding everything.
        while (lo < hi)
        {
            long cap = lo + (hi - lo) / 2;

            if (PiecesNeeded(nums, cap) <= k) hi = cap;   // feasible: try smaller
            else lo = cap + 1;
        }

        return (int)lo;
    }

    // Greedy: extend the current piece while it fits. This minimises the piece count.
    private static int PiecesNeeded(int[] nums, long cap)
    {
        int pieces = 1;
        long running = 0;

        foreach (int n in nums)
        {
            if (running + n > cap) { pieces++; running = n; }
            else running += n;
        }

        return pieces;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(SplitArray(new[] { 7, 2, 5, 10, 8 }, 2));
        Console.WriteLine(SplitArray(new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }, 2));
        Console.WriteLine(SplitArray(new[] { 1, 4, 4 }, 3));
        Console.WriteLine(SplitArray(new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }, 1));   // everything together
        Console.WriteLine(SplitArray(new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }, 5));   // largest element
        Console.WriteLine(SplitArray(new[] { 10 }, 1));
    }
}
