**Approach**

Sliding window with a count map. Grow the right edge; whenever a third distinct value appears, shrink from the left until one value's count reaches zero and it is removed.

**Logic**

The window is always valid after the shrink loop, so its width at every step is a candidate answer. Each index enters and leaves the window at most once, giving linear time despite the nested loop.

Removing the key when its count hits zero is essential — leaving a zero-count entry keeps the map's size at three and the loop shrinks forever.

O(n) time, O(1) space since the map holds at most three entries.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int LongestWithTwoDistinct(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length == 0) return 0;

        var counts = new Dictionary<int, int>();
        int left = 0, best = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < nums.Length; right++)
        {
            counts[nums[right]] = counts.GetValueOrDefault(nums[right]) + 1;

            while (counts.Count > 2)
            {
                int leaving = nums[left++];

                // Removing at zero is what actually shrinks the distinct count.
                if (--counts[leaving] == 0) counts.Remove(leaving);
            }

            best = Math.Max(best, right - left + 1);
        }

        return best;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(LongestWithTwoDistinct(new[] { 1, 2, 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestWithTwoDistinct(new[] { 0, 1, 2, 2 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestWithTwoDistinct(new[] { 1, 2, 3, 2, 2 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestWithTwoDistinct(new[] { 3, 3, 3, 1, 2, 1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 4 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestWithTwoDistinct(new[] { 5 }));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestWithTwoDistinct(Array.Empty<int>()));
        Console.WriteLine(LongestWithTwoDistinct(new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }));
    }
}
