13
6
0
0
0
```

The first two lines are the same prices with and without a fee: 13 becomes 8, because the fee makes several of the small gains not worth taking.

**Approach**

DP over transaction counts, with two states per count: currently holding a stock, or currently free. When k is large enough to be unconstrained, fall back to the unlimited-transaction greedy.

**Logic**

The large-k shortcut matters for more than speed. A transaction needs at least two days, so k above n/2 is no constraint at all, and the DP table would be needlessly enormous.

For the constrained case, holding after j transactions is either still holding from yesterday, or buying today from the free state. Being free is either still free, or selling today — and selling is what completes a transaction and advances j.

O(n·k) time, O(k) space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static int MaxProfit(int k, int[] prices)
    {
        if (prices == null || prices.Length < 2 || k == 0) return 0;

        // A transaction needs two days, so beyond n/2 the limit binds nothing.
        if (k >= prices.Length / 2)
        {
            int total = 0;
            for (int i = 1; i < prices.Length; i++)
                if (prices[i] > prices[i - 1]) total += prices[i] - prices[i - 1];
            return total;
        }

        var holding = new int[k + 1];
        var free = new int[k + 1];
        for (int j = 0; j <= k; j++) holding[j] = int.MinValue / 2;

        foreach (int price in prices)
            for (int j = 1; j <= k; j++)
            {
                // Selling is what completes a transaction, so it reads holding[j].
                free[j] = Math.Max(free[j], holding[j] + price);
                holding[j] = Math.Max(holding[j], free[j - 1] - price);
            }

        return free[k];
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProfit(2, new[] { 2, 4, 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProfit(2, new[] { 3, 2, 6, 5, 0, 3 }));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProfit(0, new[] { 1, 3 }));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProfit(1, new[] { 7, 6, 4, 3, 1 }));       // never profitable
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProfit(100, new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 }));     // unconstrained
        Console.WriteLine(MaxProfit(2, new[] { 1, 2, 4, 2, 5, 7, 2, 4, 9, 0 }));
    }
}
```

