**Approach**

A queue of candidates plus a frequency table. Push each new character onto the queue, then discard from the front while the front character has been seen more than once.

**Logic**

The queue preserves arrival order, so its front is always the earliest character that has not yet been disqualified. A character is only ever removed once, when it becomes repeated, so the total work across the stream is linear.

Discarding lazily — at query time rather than when the duplicate arrives — is what keeps each insertion O(1) amortised. Searching the counts array on every query would be O(26) per character, which is fine here but does not generalise to a large alphabet.

O(n) total.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

public class FirstNonRepeatingStream
{
    private readonly Dictionary<char, int> _counts = new();
    private readonly Queue<char> _candidates = new();

    public char Add(char c)
    {
        _counts[c] = _counts.GetValueOrDefault(c) + 1;
        _candidates.Enqueue(c);

        // Lazily discard anything that has since become repeated.
        while (_candidates.Count > 0 && _counts[_candidates.Peek()] > 1)
            _candidates.Dequeue();

        return _candidates.Count > 0 ? _candidates.Peek() : '#';
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static string Stream(string input)
    {
        var tracker = new FirstNonRepeatingStream();
        var output = new StringBuilder();

        foreach (char c in input) output.Append(tracker.Add(c));
        return output.ToString();
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(Stream("aabc"));
        Console.WriteLine(Stream("aaaa"));       // never any candidate after the first
        Console.WriteLine(Stream("abcabc"));
        Console.WriteLine(Stream("zz"));
        Console.WriteLine(Stream("a"));
        Console.WriteLine(Stream("geeksforgeeks"));
    }
}
