**Approach**

Two pointers with backtracking, remembering the last asterisk seen. The DP is O(m·n) in both time and space; this is O(m·n) worst case but O(1) space and linear in practice.

**Logic**

Walk the string and pattern together. On a literal or question-mark match, advance both. On an asterisk, record its position and the string position it was assumed to match zero characters at, then advance only the pattern.

On a mismatch, if an asterisk was seen, backtrack: let it absorb one more character and retry. That is what makes the greedy correct — the asterisk starts minimal and grows only when forced.

Trailing asterisks in the pattern must be consumed at the end, since they may match nothing.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static bool IsMatch(string s, string p)
    {
        int i = 0, j = 0;
        int starPattern = -1, starString = 0;

        while (i < s.Length)
        {
            if (j < p.Length && (p[j] == '?' || p[j] == s[i])) { i++; j++; }
            else if (j < p.Length && p[j] == '*')
            {
                // Assume it matches nothing for now; remember where to grow it.
                starPattern = j++;
                starString = i;
            }
            else if (starPattern >= 0)
            {
                // Mismatch: let the last star absorb one more character and retry.
                j = starPattern + 1;
                i = ++starString;
            }
            else return false;
        }

        // Trailing stars may match nothing, so consume them.
        while (j < p.Length && p[j] == '*') j++;

        return j == p.Length;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("aa", "a"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("aa", "*"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("cb", "?a"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("adceb", "*a*b"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("acdcb", "a*c?b"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("", "*"));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("", ""));
        Console.WriteLine(IsMatch("abc", "abc**"));
    }
}
