**Logic**

A trailing zero comes from a factor of ten, which is two times five. In any factorial, factors of two are far more plentiful than factors of five, so the count of fives is the binding constraint.

Count them by repeated division: `n/5` numbers contribute at least one five, `n/25` contribute a second, `n/125` a third, and so on. Summing those gives the total.

O(log n) time, and it never computes the factorial itself, which would overflow almost immediately.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static long TrailingZeroes(long n)
    {
        if (n < 0) throw new ArgumentOutOfRangeException(nameof(n));

        long count = 0;

        // n/5 numbers give one five, n/25 give another, and so on.
        for (long divisor = 5; divisor <= n; divisor *= 5)
            count += n / divisor;

        return count;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(TrailingZeroes(3));       // 6, no zeros
        Console.WriteLine(TrailingZeroes(5));       // 120
        Console.WriteLine(TrailingZeroes(10));      // 3628800
        Console.WriteLine(TrailingZeroes(25));      // 25 contributes two fives
        Console.WriteLine(TrailingZeroes(0));
        Console.WriteLine(TrailingZeroes(1000000000000));
    }
}
```

