4
0
0
7
2
```

**Logic**

Treat every zero as -1. A subarray with equal counts then sums to zero, so the question becomes the longest subarray with sum zero, which prefix sums answer.

Keep a map from running sum to the earliest index at which it occurred. If the same sum appears again later, everything between the two positions sums to zero. Seed the map with sum 0 at index -1 so a qualifying subarray starting at index 0 is measured correctly.

Recording only the first occurrence is what makes the span maximal. O(n) time and space.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class Solution
{
    public static int FindMaxLength(int[] nums)
    {
        if (nums == null || nums.Length == 0) return 0;

        // sum -> FIRST index where it occurred. Seeded so a run from index 0 works.
        var firstSeen = new Dictionary<int, int> { [0] = -1 };

        int sum = 0, best = 0;

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            sum += nums[i] == 0 ? -1 : 1;    // zero counts as -1

            if (firstSeen.TryGetValue(sum, out int earlier))
                best = Math.Max(best, i - earlier);
            else
                firstSeen[sum] = i;           // only the first occurrence is recorded
        }

        return best;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(FindMaxLength(new[] { 0, 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(FindMaxLength(new[] { 0, 1, 0 }));
        Console.WriteLine(FindMaxLength(new[] { 0, 0, 1, 0, 0, 0, 1, 1 }));
        Console.WriteLine(FindMaxLength(new[] { 1, 1, 1 }));      // never balances
        Console.WriteLine(FindMaxLength(new[] { 0, 1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 0 }));
        Console.WriteLine(FindMaxLength(new[] { 0 }));
    }
}
