**Logic**

A single tracked maximum fails because popping it must restore the previous maximum, which needs history. A parallel stack whose top is always the current maximum provides exactly that.

The space-efficient form pushes onto the auxiliary stack only when the incoming value is greater than or equal to the current maximum, and pops only when the removed value equals the top. The `equal to` matters: with two copies of the maximum, recording only one would report a stale maximum after the first pop.

All operations are O(1).

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class MaxStack
{
    private readonly Stack<int> _values = new();
    private readonly Stack<int> _maxima = new();

    public void Push(int value)
    {
        _values.Push(value);

        // >= so that duplicates of the maximum are each recorded.
        if (_maxima.Count == 0 || value >= _maxima.Peek()) _maxima.Push(value);
    }

    public int Pop()
    {
        if (_values.Count == 0) throw new InvalidOperationException("stack is empty");

        int value = _values.Pop();
        if (_maxima.Count > 0 && value == _maxima.Peek()) _maxima.Pop();

        return value;
    }

    public int Peek() => _values.Count > 0
        ? _values.Peek()
        : throw new InvalidOperationException("stack is empty");

    public int Max() => _maxima.Count > 0
        ? _maxima.Peek()
        : throw new InvalidOperationException("stack is empty");

    public int Count => _values.Count;
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var s = new MaxStack();

        s.Push(5);
        s.Push(1);
        s.Push(5);        // duplicate maximum

        Console.WriteLine(s.Max());
        s.Pop();
        Console.WriteLine(s.Max());   // still 5: the duplicate was recorded

        s.Pop();
        Console.WriteLine(s.Max());
        Console.WriteLine(s.Count);

        s.Push(-3);
        Console.WriteLine(s.Max());   // negatives do not disturb the maximum

        s.Pop(); s.Pop();
        try { s.Max(); }
        catch (InvalidOperationException e) { Console.WriteLine(e.Message); }
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
5
5
5
1
5
stack is empty
```

**Logic**

A string or list makes insertion at the cursor O(n), because everything after it shifts. The classic fix is two stacks: one holding the text before the cursor and one holding the text after it, reversed.

Insertion pushes onto the left stack, which is O(1). Moving the cursor transfers characters between the stacks, one per step. Deletion pops the left stack. The text is the left stack in order followed by the right stack reversed.

That is the gap buffer idea, and it is exactly why editors are fast at typing: the expensive operation is long-distance cursor movement, which is rare, while typing is constant time.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Text;

public class TextEditor
{
    private readonly Stack<char> _left = new();    // text before the cursor
    private readonly Stack<char> _right = new();   // text after, reversed

    public void Insert(string text)
    {
        foreach (char c in text) _left.Push(c);    // O(1) per character
    }

    // Delete up to k characters before the cursor; returns how many went.
    public int DeleteLeft(int k)
    {
        int deleted = 0;
        while (k-- > 0 && _left.Count > 0) { _left.Pop(); deleted++; }
        return deleted;
    }

    public int CursorLeft(int k)
    {
        int moved = 0;
        while (k-- > 0 && _left.Count > 0) { _right.Push(_left.Pop()); moved++; }
        return moved;
    }

    public int CursorRight(int k)
    {
        int moved = 0;
        while (k-- > 0 && _right.Count > 0) { _left.Push(_right.Pop()); moved++; }
        return moved;
    }

    // The last min(10, n) characters before the cursor, as the problem specifies.
    public string TextBeforeCursor(int count = 10)
    {
        var buffer = new List<char>();
        foreach (char c in _left)                   // Stack enumerates top-first
        {
            if (buffer.Count == count) break;
            buffer.Add(c);
        }
        buffer.Reverse();
        return new string(buffer.ToArray());
    }

    public override string ToString()
    {
        var sb = new StringBuilder();
        var leftInOrder = new List<char>(_left);
        leftInOrder.Reverse();
        foreach (char c in leftInOrder) sb.Append(c);
        sb.Append('|');                             // cursor marker
        foreach (char c in _right) sb.Append(c);
        return sb.ToString();
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        var editor = new TextEditor();

        editor.Insert("leetcode");
        Console.WriteLine(editor.ToString());

        Console.WriteLine(editor.DeleteLeft(4));
        Console.WriteLine(editor.ToString());

        editor.Insert("practice");
        Console.WriteLine(editor.CursorLeft(8));
        Console.WriteLine(editor.ToString());

        Console.WriteLine(editor.TextBeforeCursor());
        Console.WriteLine(editor.CursorRight(100));   // clamps at the end
        Console.WriteLine(editor.ToString());
    }
}
```

**Output**

```
leetcode|
4
leet|
8
leet|practice
leet
8
leetpractice|
```

**Logic**

Two heaps give a streaming median, but a window also needs *removal* of the element leaving on the left, which a heap cannot do efficiently for an arbitrary element.

The standard fix is lazy deletion: keep a map of values pending removal, and discard them only when they surface at a heap root. Effective sizes are tracked separately from the heaps' raw counts, since the heaps contain entries that are logically gone.

For clarity here, a SortedList of value-to-count gives the same answer with simpler removal, at O(n) per shift in the worst case but with no bookkeeping. Which to choose depends on whether k is large.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

public class SlidingWindowMedian
{
    // Value -> how many copies are currently in the window. Sorted by value.
    private readonly SortedDictionary<int, int> _window = new();
    private int _count;

    public void Add(int value)
    {
        _window[value] = _window.TryGetValue(value, out int c) ? c + 1 : 1;
        _count++;
    }

    public void Remove(int value)
    {
        if (!_window.TryGetValue(value, out int c))
            throw new InvalidOperationException($"{value} is not in the window");

        if (c == 1) _window.Remove(value);   // drop the key entirely at zero
        else _window[value] = c - 1;

        _count--;
    }

    public double Median()
    {
        if (_count == 0) throw new InvalidOperationException("window is empty");

        // Walk the sorted keys accumulating counts to find the middle position(s).
        int lowerTarget = (_count - 1) / 2;
        int upperTarget = _count / 2;

        int seen = 0;
        long lower = 0, upper = 0;
        bool haveLower = false;

        foreach (var (value, count) in _window)
        {
            seen += count;

            if (!haveLower && seen > lowerTarget) { lower = value; haveLower = true; }
            if (seen > upperTarget) { upper = value; break; }
        }

        return (lower + upper) / 2.0;
    }

    public IList<double> Medians(int[] nums, int k)
    {
        var result = new List<double>();

        for (int i = 0; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            Add(nums[i]);

            if (i >= k) Remove(nums[i - k]);
            if (i >= k - 1) result.Add(Median());
        }

        return result;
    }
}

public class Solution
{
    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", new SlidingWindowMedian()
            .Medians(new[] { 1, 3, -1, -3, 5, 3, 6, 7 }, 3)));

        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", new SlidingWindowMedian()
            .Medians(new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4 }, 2)));        // even windows average two values

        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", new SlidingWindowMedian()
            .Medians(new[] { 5, 5, 5 }, 3)));           // duplicates

        Console.WriteLine(string.Join(",", new SlidingWindowMedian()
            .Medians(new[] { 7 }, 1)));

        var m = new SlidingWindowMedian();
        try { m.Median(); }
        catch (InvalidOperationException e) { Console.WriteLine(e.Message); }
    }
}
```

