8,5,4
4
10,8,5
3
only 1 of 5 elements seen
```

**Logic**

A fixed-size sliding window. Sum the first k elements, then slide: add the entering element and subtract the leaving one, keeping a running maximum. Each step is O(1), so the whole scan is O(n).

Recomputing the sum for every window would be O(n·k), which is the naive version this replaces. This is the simplest sliding-window problem and the template for every fixed-window variant.

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    public static long MaxSumOfK(int[] nums, int k)
    {
        if (nums == null || k <= 0 || k > nums.Length)
            throw new ArgumentException("k must be between 1 and the array length");

        long sum = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < k; i++) sum += nums[i];

        long best = sum;

        for (int i = k; i < nums.Length; i++)
        {
            sum += nums[i] - nums[i - k];    // one in, one out: O(1) per step
            best = Math.Max(best, sum);
        }

        return best;
    }

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine(MaxSumOfK(new[] { 2, 1, 5, 1, 3, 2 }, 3));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxSumOfK(new[] { 2, 3, 4, 1, 5 }, 2));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxSumOfK(new[] { -1, -2, -3 }, 2));     // all negative
        Console.WriteLine(MaxSumOfK(new[] { 5 }, 1));
        Console.WriteLine(MaxSumOfK(new[] { 1, 2, 3, 4 }, 4));     // the whole array

        try { MaxSumOfK(new[] { 1, 2 }, 5); }
        catch (ArgumentException) { Console.WriteLine("k too large"); }
    }
}
```

