**Logic**

A power of two has exactly one set bit, so `n & (n - 1)` clears it and leaves zero. Guard against zero and negatives first, since zero passes the bit test but is not a power of two.

A power of four is a power of two whose single bit sits at an even position. Two ways to check: mask against `0x55555555`, which has bits only at even positions, or note that powers of four leave remainder one modulo three.

Both tests are O(1).

**Program**

```csharp
using System;

public class Solution
{
    // Exactly one set bit, and positive.
    public static bool IsPowerOfTwo(int n) => n > 0 && (n & (n - 1)) == 0;

    // A power of two whose bit sits at an even position.
    public static bool IsPowerOfFour(int n) =>
        IsPowerOfTwo(n) && (n & 0x55555555) != 0;

    // Alternative: powers of four are congruent to 1 modulo 3.
    public static bool IsPowerOfFourMod(int n) =>
        IsPowerOfTwo(n) && n % 3 == 1;

    public static void Main()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"{IsPowerOfTwo(1)} {IsPowerOfTwo(16)} {IsPowerOfTwo(0)} {IsPowerOfTwo(-16)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"{IsPowerOfTwo(3)} {IsPowerOfTwo(1073741824)}");

        Console.WriteLine($"{IsPowerOfFour(1)} {IsPowerOfFour(4)} {IsPowerOfFour(8)} {IsPowerOfFour(16)}");
        Console.WriteLine($"{IsPowerOfFourMod(1)} {IsPowerOfFourMod(4)} {IsPowerOfFourMod(8)} {IsPowerOfFourMod(16)}");
        Console.WriteLine(IsPowerOfFour(0));
        Console.WriteLine(IsPowerOfFour(64));
    }
}
```

